{
  "dom_id": "step-10423",
  "name": "Applications to Squares",
  "problem": "Now that we know about $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles, it's straightforward to see that the diagonal of any square is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side length of the square.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the square below, where $a$ is the measure of a side and $c$ is the measure of a diagonal.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182820/assets/0743e533eefd4c7e51eaca51c9285fc1b6277553d4f214602a65fc50d1d69ace.png)\n\nSince the sides of a square are congruent, $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an isosceles right triangle (a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle). \n\nSo, for the diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
